"فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "لَءَايَٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَٰyāَءَl", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "أَفَلَا", "translit": "āَlَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "يَسْمَعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعَmْsَy", "root": "سمع"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 27, "text": "أَوَلَمْ يَرَوْا۟ أَنَّا نَسُوقُ ٱلْمَآءَ إِلَى ٱلْأَرْضِ ٱلْجُرُزِ فَنُخْرِجُ بِهِۦ زَرْعًا تَأْكُلُ مِنْهُ أَنْعَٰمُهُمْ وَأَنفُسُهُمْۖ أَفَلَا يُبْصِرُونَ ", "translation": "Have they not seen that We drive water [in clouds] to barren land and bring forth thereby crops from which their livestock eat and [they] themselves? Then do they not see?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَوَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَرَوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَrَy", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنَّا", "translit": "āَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "نَسُوقُ", "translit": "ُqwُsَn", "root": "سوق"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْمَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَmْlٱ", "root": "موه"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِلَى", "translit": "àَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْجُرُزِ", "translit": "ِzُrُjgْlٱ", "root": "جرز"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَنُخْرِجُ", "translit": "ُjgِrْhkُnَf", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "زَرْعًا", "translit": "āًʿعْrَz", "root": "زرع"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "تَأْكُلُ", "translit": "ُlُkْaʾَt", "root": "اكل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مِنْهُ", "translit": "ُhْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "أَنْعَٰمُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُmَٰʿعْnَaʾ", "root": "نعم"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَأَنفُسُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُsُfnَaʾَw", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "أَفَلَا", "translit": "āَlَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "يُبْصِرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrِṣْbُy", "root": "بصر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 28, "text": "وَيَقُولُونَ مَتَىٰ هَٰذَا ٱلْفَتْحُ إِن كُنتُمْ صَٰدِقِينَ ", "translation": "And they say, \"When will be this conquest,[1] if you should be truthful?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَيَقُولُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlwُqَyَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَتَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَtَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْفَتْحُ", "translit": "ُḥْtَfْlٱ", "root": "فتح"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "كُنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "صَٰدِقِينَ", "translit": "َnyِqِdَٰṣ", "root": "صدق"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 29, "text": "قُلْ يَوْمَ ٱلْفَتْحِ لَا يَنفَعُ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوٓا۟ إِيمَٰنُهُمْ وَلَا هُمْ يُنظَرُونَ ", "translation": "Say, [O Muḥammad], \"On the Day of Conquest the belief of those who had disbelieved will not benefit them, nor will they be reprieved.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْفَتْحِ", "translit": "ِḥْtَfْlٱ", "root": "فتح"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَنفَعُ", "translit": "ُʿعَfnَy", "root": "نفع"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كَفَرُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِيمَٰنُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُnَٰmyِiʾ", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يُنظَرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrَẓnُy", "root": "نظر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 30, "text": "فَأَعْرِضْ عَنْهُمْ وَٱنتَظِرْ إِنَّهُم مُّنتَظِرُونَ ", "translation": "So turn away from them and wait. Indeed, they are waiting.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَأَعْرِضْ", "translit": "ْḍِrْʿعَaʾَf", "root": "عرض"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَنْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَٱنتَظِرْ", "translit": "ْrِẓَtnٱَw", "root": "نظر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِنَّهُم", "translit": "mُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مُّنتَظِرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrِẓَtnُّm", "root": "نظر"}], "hasIhya": false}], "tafsir": {"7": [{"arabic": "الثاني أن يعرف نسبه الحقيقي، فيعرف أباه وجده فإن أباه القريب نطفة قذرة وجده البعيد تراب ذليل وقد عرفه الله تعالى نسبه فقال \"الذين أحسن كل شيء خلقه وبدأ خلق الإنسان من طين ثم جعل نسله من سلالة من ماء مهين\" فمن أصله التراب المهين الذي يداس بالأقدام ثم خمر طينة حتى صار حمأ مسنوناً كيف يتكبر؟ وأخس الأشياء ما إليه انتسابه إذ يقال يا أذل من التراب ويا أنتن من الحمأة ويا أقذر من المضغة.", "english": "The second is that he should know his true lineage, so he knows his father and grandfather, for his close father is a filthy drop (Nutfah) and his distant grandfather is lowly dust (Turab), and Allah the Exalted has made known his lineage when He said: \"The One Who perfected everything He created and began the creation of the human being from clay, then made his progeny from an extract of a despised fluid.\" So from his origin being the despised dust (Turab) that is trodden under feet, then He fermented the clay until it became stinking mud (Hama'), how can he be arrogant? And the most vile things are what he traces his lineage to, for it is said: O more lowly than dust, O more foul-smelling than stinking mud, and O more filthy than a chewed lump (Mudghah).", "book_file": "vol3_Vol3-book-9a.doc.txt", "book_title": "Condemnation of Pride and Conceit", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the virtue and practice of remembering God, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'", "badge": "Dhikr"}], "11": [{"arabic": "ولأجل توافق ذلك وتطابقه نسب الله تعالى الأفعال في القرآن مرة إلى الملائكة ومرة إلى العباد، ونسبها بعينها مرة أخرى إلى نفسه، فقال الله تعالى في الموت \" قل يتوفاكم ملك الموت \" ثم قال عز وجل: \" الله يتوفى الأنفس حين موتها \" وقال تعالى: \" أفرأيتم ما تحرثون \" أضاف إلينا ثم قال تعالى: \" أنا صببنا ثم شققنا الأرض شقاً فأنبتنا فيها حباً وعنباً \"، وقال عز وجل: \" فأرسلنا إليها روحنا فتمثل لها بشراً سوياً \"، ثم قال تعالى: \" فنفخنا فيها من روحنا \" وكان النافخ جبريل عليه السلام، وكما قال تعالى: \" فإذا قرأناه ", "english": "And due to the correspondence and congruence of that, Allah the Exalted attributed actions in the Quran at one time to the angels and at another time to the servants, and attributed the very same actions at another time to Himself. So Allah the Exalted said regarding death: \"Say: 'The Angel of Death (Malak al-Mawt) who is entrusted with you will take your souls (yatawaffākum)'\" then He the Exalted said: \"Allah takes the souls (yatawaffā) at the time of their death.\" And He the Exalted said: \"Have you considered what you sow?\" He attributed it to us, then He the Exalted said: \"We pour down water, then We split the earth in fissures and cause to grow therein grain and grapes.\" And He the Exalted said: \"So We sent to her Our Spirit (Rūḥ), and he represented himself to her as a well-proportioned man.\" Then He the Exalted said: \"So We breathed into her of Our Spirit (Rūḥ),\" and the one breathing was Gabriel, peace be upon him. And as He the Exalted said: \"So when We have recited it, then follow its recitation,\" it is said in the interpretation (tafsīr): its meaning is when Gabriel recites it to you. And He the Exalted said: \"Fight them; Allah will punish them by your hands,\" so He attributed the killing to them and the punishment to Himself, and the punishment is the very killing. Indeed, He made it explicit and said: \"So you did not kill them, but Allah killed them.\" And He the Exalted said: \"And you did not throw when you threw, but Allah threw,\" and it is a combination of negation and affirmation apparently, but its meaning is: and you did not throw by the meaning by which the Lord is a thrower, when you threw by the meaning by which the servant is a thrower, for they are two different meanings.", "book_file": "vol4_Vol4-book5.doc.txt", "book_title": "Monotheism and Reliance", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's insights on the inner dimensions of faith, from 'Monotheism and Reliance'", "badge": "Insight"}], "12": [{"arabic": "ومن عدا هؤلاء فهم المغرورون بالله \"وسوف يعلمون حين يرون العذاب من أضل سبيل-ولتعلمن نبأه بعد حين\" وعند ذلك يقولون كما أخبر الله عنهم \"ربنا أبصرنا وسمعنا فارجعنا نعمل صالحاً إنا موقنون\" أي علمنا أنه كما لا يولد إلا بوقاع ونكاح ولا ينبت زرع إلا بحراثة وبث بذر، فكذلك لا يحصل في الآخرة ثواب وأجر إلا بعمل صالح فارجعنا نعمل صالحاً فقد علمنا الآن صدقك في قولك \"وأن ليس للإنسان إلا ما سعى وأن سعيه سوف يرى -كلما ألقى فيها فوج سألهم خزنتها ألم يأتكم نذير قالوا بلى قد جاءنا نذير\" أي ألم نسمعكم سنة الله في عب", "english": "And whoever is other than these are those deluded by Allah \"And they will know when they see the punishment who is more astray in way - and you will know its news after a while.\" And at that time they will say as Allah informed about them: \"Our Lord, we have seen and heard, so return us, we will work righteousness, indeed we are certain.\" That is, we have known that just as a child is not born except by intercourse and marriage, and a crop does not grow except by plowing and sowing seed, likewise there is no attainment in the Hereafter of reward and recompense except by righteous work, so return us, we will work righteousness, for we have now known the truthfulness in Your saying: \"And that for man is only what he strives for, and that his striving will be seen - whenever a group is thrown into it, its keepers ask them: 'Did there not come to you a warner?' They say: 'Yes, a warner had come to us.'\" That is, did we not hear you about the Sunnah (established way) of Allah among His servants and that \"He takes every soul what it has earned\" and that \"Every soul is a pledge for what it has earned\"? So what deluded you about Allah after you heard and comprehended? \"They said: 'If we had been hearing or comprehending, we would not have been among the companions of the Blaze.' So they confessed their sin, so away with the companions of the Blaze.\"", "book_file": "vol3_Vol3-book-10a.doc.txt", "book_title": "Condemnation of Delusion", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's insights on the inner dimensions of faith, from 'Condemnation of Delusion'", "badge": "Insight"}], "13": [{"arabic": "ثم يخبر عنه ويقول: ﴿ ولو شئنا لآتينا كل نفس هداها ولكن حق القول مني لأملأن جهنم من الجنة والناس أجمعين ﴾ {} وقال تعالى: ﴿ وتمت كلمة ربك لأملأن جهنم ﴾ {} الآية، فكيف لا يخاف ما حق من القول في الأزل ولا يطمع في تداركه ولو كان الأمر أنفا لكانت الأطماع تمتد إلى حيلة فيه ولكن ليس إلا التسليم فيه واستقراء خفى السابقة من جلى الأسباب الظاهرة على القلب والجوارح فمن يسرت له أسباب الشر وحيل بينه وبين أسباب الخير وأحكمت علاقته من الدنيا فكأنه كشف له على التحقيق سر السابقة التي سبقت له بالشقاوة، إذ كل ميسر ل", "english": "Then He informs about it and says: 'And if We had willed, We could have given every Soul (Nafs) its guidance, but the Word (Qawl) from Me has been decreed: I will surely fill Hell with jinn and mankind all together.' And the Exalted said: 'And the Word (Kalimah) of your Lord has been fulfilled: I will surely fill Hell...' the verse. So how can one not fear what has been decreed from the Word (Qawl) in eternity and not hope to recover it? If the matter were otherwise, hopes would extend to a stratagem in it, but there is nothing except submission (Taslim) in it and investigating the hidden precedence from the apparent causes manifest on the Heart (Qalb) and limbs. For whoever is facilitated for him the causes of evil and is prevented between him and the causes of good, and his connection from the world is firmly established, it is as if the secret of the precedence that preceded for him with wretchedness has been revealed to him in truth, since everyone is facilitated for what he was created for. And if all good deeds are facilitated and the Heart (Qalb) is completely cut off from the world and with its outward and inward turned towards God, this would necessitate alleviation of fear if constancy upon that were trusted, but the danger of the ending and the difficulty of steadfastness increase the flames of fear in ignition and cannot be extinguished. And how can one feel secure from changing of state when the Heart (Qalb) of the believer is between two fingers of the fingers of the Most Merciful, and the Heart (Qalb) is more fluctuating than the pot in its boiling? And the Turner of Hearts (Muqallib al-Qulub), may He be glorified, said: 'Indeed, the punishment of their Lord is not to be secure from.' So the most ignorant of people is he who feels secure from it while He calls out with warning from security. And if God had been gentle with His servants, the knowers, when He revived their hearts with the spirit of hope, their hearts would have burned from the fire of fear. So the causes of hope are a mercy for the elect...", "book_file": "vol4_Vol4-book3.doc.txt", "book_title": "Fear and Hope", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's insights on the inner dimensions of faith, from 'Fear and Hope'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "الحالة الثانية أن تغلب دواعي الهوى وتسقط بالكلية منازعة باعث الدين فيسلم نفسه إلى جند الشياطين ولا يجاهد ليأسه من المجاهدة وهؤلاء هم الغافلون وهم الأكثرون وهم الذين استرقتهم شهواتهم وغلبت عليهم شقوتهم فحكموا أعداء الله في قلوبهم التي هي سر من أسرار الله تعالى وأمر من أمور الله وإليهم الإشارة بقوله تعالى: ﴿ ولو شئنا لآتينا كل نفس هداها ولكن حق القول مني لأملأن جهنم من الجنة والناس أجمعين ﴾ {} وهؤلاء هم الذين اشتروا الحياة الدنيا بالآخرة فخسرت صفقتهم وقيل لمن قصد إرشادهم ﴿ فأعرض عمن تولى عن ذكرنا ", "english": "The second state is that the incitements of desire (Hawa) overcome and the urging of religion (Din) completely ceases to contend, so he surrenders his soul (Nafs) to the armies of the devils and does not struggle due to his despair from struggling. These are the heedless ones (Ghaafiloon) and they are the majority, and they are those whose lusts have enslaved them and their wretchedness has overcome them, so they have given authority to the enemies of Allah in their hearts (Quloob), which are a secret (Sirr) from the secrets of Allah the Exalted and a matter (Amr) from the affairs of Allah. To them is the indication by His saying, the Exalted: 'And if We had willed, We could have given every soul (Nafs) its guidance, but the word (Qawl) from Me will come true: \"I will surely fill Hell with jinn and mankind all together.\"' And these are those who have purchased the worldly life (Al-Hayaat Al-Dunya) with the Hereafter (Al-Aakhirah), so their transaction has incurred loss, and it is said to one who intends to guide them: 'So turn away from he who turns away from Our remembrance (Dhikr) and desires not except the worldly life. That is their extent of knowledge.' And this state's sign is despair (Yaa's) and hopelessness (Qunoot) and delusion with hopes (Amaani), which is the utmost foolishness (Hamaq), as it is said: 'The intelligent one (Al-Kayyis) is he who holds his soul (Nafs) accountable and works for what is after death, and the foolish one (Al-Ahmaq) is he who follows his soul (Nafs) its desire (Hawa) and hopes upon Allah.' And the possessor of this state, when admonished, says: 'I am longing for repentance (Tawbah), but it has become impossible for me, so I do not hope for it,' or he was not longing for repentance (Tawbah) but said: 'Indeed Allah is Forgiving, Merciful, Generous, so He has no need for my repentance.' And this wretched one has become his intellect (Aql) weak for his lust (Shahwah), so he does not use his intellect (Aql) except in deducing subtle tricks by which he attains the fulfillment of his lust (Shahwah). Indeed, his intellect (Aql) has become in the hand of his lusts (Shahawaat) like a Muslim captive in the hands of the disbelievers (Kuffaar), so they enslave him in tending pigs, guarding wines, and carrying them. And his place with Allah the Exalted is the place of one who overpowers a Muslim and delivers him to the disbelievers (Kuffaar) and makes him a captive with them, because by his atrocious crime he resembles one who enslaved what had the right not to be enslaved and gave authority to what had the right not to have authority over it. And indeed, the Muslim deserved to be dominant because of what is in him of knowledge (Ma'rifah) of Allah and the urging of religion (Din), and indeed, the disbeliever (Kaafir) deserved to be...", "book_file": "vol4_Vol4-book2.doc.txt", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's insights on the inner dimensions of faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "فإن قلت: فلو ترك الوعاظ الوعظ إلا عند نيل هذه الدرجة لخلت الدنيا عن الوعظ خربت القلوب؟ فأقول قد قال رسول الله ﭬ \"حب الدنيا رأس كل خطيئة\"[36] ولو لم يحب الناس الدنيا لهلك العالم وبطلت المعايش وهلكت القلوب والأبدان جميعاً، إلا أنه ﭬ علم أن حب الدنيا مهلك وأن ذكر كونه مهلكاً لا ينزع الحب من قلوب الأكثرين لا الأقلين الذين لا تخرب الدنيا بتركهم، فلم يترك النصح وذكر ما في حب الدنيا من الخطر ولم يترك ذكره خوفاً من أن يترك نفسه بالشهوات المهلكة التي سلطها الله على عباده ليسوقهم بها إلى جهنم تصديقاً لقول", "english": "If you say: If preachers were to abandon preaching except upon attaining this degree, then the world would be devoid of preaching and hearts would be ruined? I say: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said, \"Love of the world (Dunya) is the head of every sin.\" And if people did not love the world, the world would perish, livelihoods would cease, and hearts and bodies would all be destroyed. However, he (peace be upon him) knew that love of the world is destructive and that mentioning its destructiveness does not remove the love from the hearts of the majority, not the minority whose abandonment would not ruin the world. So he did not abandon advising and mentioning the danger in love of the world, nor did he abandon mentioning it out of fear that he would leave himself to the destructive desires that Allah has empowered over His servants to drive them with them to Hellfire, in confirmation of His saying, the Most High: \"But the word from Me has become true: I will fill Hell with jinn and mankind all together.\" Likewise, the tongues of preachers remain unleashed for love of leadership, and they do not abandon it due to the saying of one who says: Preaching for love of leadership is forbidden, just as the creation does not abandon drinking, fornication, theft, hypocrisy, oppression, and other sins due to the saying of Allah, the Most High, and His Messenger that it is forbidden. So look to your own self (Nafs) and be empty-hearted (Qalb) from the talk of people, for Allah, the Most High, reforms many creation through the corruption of one person and persons: \"And if Allah did not repel some people by others, the earth would be corrupted.\" And Allah supports this religion with people who have no share (in the Hereafter). What is feared is that the path of taking admonition is corrupted. As for silencing the tongues of preachers while behind them is the motive of leadership and love of the world, that will never be.", "book_file": "vol3_Vol3-book-10a.doc.txt", "book_title": "Condemnation of Delusion", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the diseases of the heart and their cure, from 'Condemnation of Delusion'", "badge": "Ethics"}, {"arabic": "الأصل الثالث: أن فعل العبد وإن كان كسبا للعبد فلا يخرج عن كونه مرادا لله سبحانه فلا يجري في الملك والملكوت طرفة عين ولا لفتة خاطر ولا فلتة ناظر إلا بقضاء الله وقدرته وبإرادته ومشيئته ومنه الشر والخير والنفع والضر والإسلام والكفر والعرفان والنكر والفوز والخسران والغواية والرشد والطاعة والعصيان والشرك والإيمان لا راد لقضائه ولا معقب لحكمه يضل من يشاء ويهدي من يشاء لا يسئل عما يفعل وهم يسألون ويدل عليه من النقل قول الأمة قاطبة ما شاء كان وما لم يشأ لم يكن وقول الله عز وجل أن لو يشاء الله لهدى الناس", "english": "The third foundation: That the action of the servant (al-'abd), even if it is an acquisition (kasb) of the servant, does not depart from being willed by Allah, glorified and exalted be He. Therefore, not a blink of an eye, nor a turn of thought, nor a glance of the eye occurs in the dominion (al-mulk) and the kingdom (al-malakūt) except by the decree (qadā') of Allah and His power (qudrah) and by His will (irādah) and His volition (mashī'ah). And from Him is evil and good, benefit and harm, Islam and disbelief (kufr), recognition (ma'rifah) and denial (inkār), success and loss, misguidance (ghawāyah) and right guidance (rushd), obedience and disobedience, polytheism (shirk) and faith (īmān). There is no repeller of His decree and no reverser of His judgment. He misguides whom He wills and guides whom He wills. He is not questioned about what He does, while they will be questioned. And what indicates this from transmitted tradition (al-naql) is the statement of the entire community: 'What He wills is, and what He does not will is not,' and the statement of Allah, mighty and majestic: 'If Allah had willed, He would have guided all the people,' and His statement, exalted: 'And if We had willed, We could have given every soul (nafs) its guidance (hudāhā).' And what indicates this from the perspective of intellect (al-'aql) is that sins (ma'āṣī) and crimes, if Allah dislikes them and does not will them, yet they occur in accordance with the will of the enemy, Iblīs—may Allah curse him—even though he is an enemy to Allah, glorified and exalted be He, and what occurs in accordance with the will of the enemy is more than what occurs in accordance with His will, exalted. I wonder how a Muslim permits himself to reduce the dominion of the Compeller (al-Jabbār), Possessor of Majesty and Honor, to a rank that, if the leadership of a village chief were reduced to it, he would disdain it. For if what continues for the enemy of the chief in the village were more than what is established for him, he would disdain his leadership and absolve himself of his authority. And sin is what predominates over creation, and all of that, according to the innovators (al-mubtadi'ah), occurs contrary to the will of the Truth (al-Ḥaqq), exalted. And this is the utmost weakness and incapacity—exalted is the Lord of lords above what the wrongdoers say, supremely great. Then, whenever it becomes apparent that the actions of the servants are created by Allah, it is sound that they are willed by Him. If it is said: 'How does He forbid what He wills and command what He does not will?' We say: Command (al-amr) is different from will (al-irādah). Therefore, when a master strikes his servant and the ruler reproaches him for it, and he excuses himself by the rebellion of his servant against him, and the ruler disbelieves him, so he wishes to manifest his proof by commanding the servant to do an action and he disobeys him in the presence of the ruler, so he says to him: 'Saddle this mount in the sight of the ruler.' Thus, he commands him with what he does not will his compliance with, and if he were not a commander, his excuse would not be facilitated before the ruler, and if he were...", "book_file": "vol1_Vol1-book-2.doc.txt", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions cited by Al-Ghazali with scholarly authentication, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "ولم يقطع التوفيق عن الكفار لجناية متقدمة ولا أمد الأنبياء عليهم السلام بتوفيقه ونور هدايته لوسيلة سابقة ولكنه قال ولقد سبقت كلمتنا لعبادنا المرسلين وقال عز وجل ولكن حق القول مني لأملأن جهنم من الجنة والناس أجمعين وقال تعالى إن الذين سبقت لهم منا الحسنى أولئك عنها مبعدون فإن خطر ببالك أنه لم اختلفت السابقة وهم في ربقه العبودية مشتركون نوديت من سرادقات الجلال لا تجاوز حد الأدب فإنه لا يسئل عما يفعل وهم يسئلون", "english": "And He did not cut off success (Tawfiq) from the disbelievers due to a preceding transgression, nor did He extend to the Prophets, peace be upon them, His success (Tawfiq) and the light (Nur) of His guidance (Hidayah) due to a prior means. Rather, He said: 'And Our word has already preceded for Our servants, the messengers.' And the Almighty said: 'But the word (Qawl) from Me has become true: I will surely fill Hell with jinn and men all together.' And the Exalted said: 'Indeed, those for whom the best (Al-Husna) from Us has preceded, they will be removed far from it.' If it occurs to your mind that the precedence (Sabiqah) did not differ while they are partners in the bond (Ribqah) of servitude (Ubudiyyah), you are called from the pavilions (Saradiqu) of majesty (Jalal): 'Do not exceed the limit of etiquette (Adab), for He is not questioned about what He does, but they will be questioned.'", "book_file": "vol2_j2-k08.doc.txt", "book_title": "Audition and Ecstasy", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions cited by Al-Ghazali with scholarly authentication, from 'Audition and Ecstasy'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "15": [{"arabic": "وأكمله أن يكبر فيسجد ويدعو في سجوده بما يليق بالآية التي قرأها مثل أن يقرأ قوله تعالى: ﴿ إِنَّمَا يُؤْمِنُ بِآيَاتِنَا الَّذِينَ إِذَا ذُكِّرُوا بِهَا خَرُّوا سُجَّدًا وَسَبَّحُوا بِحَمْدِ رَبِّهِمْ وَهُمْ لَا يَسْتَكْبِرُونَ ﴾ فيقول اللهم اجعلني من الساجدين لوجهك المسبحين بحمدك وأعوذ بك أن أكون من المستكبرين عن أمرك أو على أوليائك", "english": "And its most complete form is that he says 'Allahu Akbar' and prostrates and supplicates in his prostration with what is appropriate to the verse he recited, such as when he recites the saying of Allah the Exalted: 'Only those believe in Our signs who, when they are reminded by them, fall down prostrating and glorify with praise of their Lord, and they are not arrogant.' Then he says: O Allah, make me among those who prostrate to Your Face (Wajh), those who glorify with Your praise, and I seek refuge in You from being among those who are arrogant about Your command or against Your allies.", "book_file": "vol1_Vol1-book-8.doc.txt", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications and their spiritual significance, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "16": [{"arabic": "ولهذه الصلاة فضل عظيم وقيل إنها المراد بقوله عز وجل تتجافى جنوبهم عن المضاجع وقد روى عنه ﷺ أنه قال: )من صلى بين المغرب والعشاء فإنها من صلاة الأوابين[241]) وقال ﷺ: (من عكف نفسه فيما بين المغرب والعشاء في مسجد جماعة لم يتكلم إلا بصلاة أو بقرآن كان حقا على الله أن يبني له قصرين في الجنة مسيرة كل قصر منهما مائة عام ويغرس له بينهما غراسا لوطافة أهل الأرض لوسعهم[242]) وسيأتي بقية فضائلها في كتاب الأوراد إن شاء الله تعالى.", "english": "And for this prayer there is great virtue, and it is said that it is what is intended by His saying, the Exalted: 'Their sides (جنوبهم) withdraw from the beds (المضاجع), calling upon their Lord in fear (خوفًا) and hope (طمعًا), and from what We have provided them they spend.' And it has been narrated from him (peace be upon him) that he said: 'Whoever prays between Maghrib and Isha, then it is from the prayer of the oft-returning ones (الأوابين).' And he (peace be upon him) said: 'Whoever confines his soul (نفسه) between Maghrib and Isha in a congregational mosque, not speaking except with prayer or Quran, it is a right upon Allah to build for him two palaces in Paradise, the journey of each palace being one hundred years, and to plant for him between them plantings that, if the delicacy of the people of the earth were to encompass them, it would suffice them.' And the remainder of its virtues will come in the Book of Regular Devotions (الأوراد), if Allah wills.", "book_file": "vol1_Vol1-book-4.doc.txt", "book_title": "Mysteries of Prayer", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's insights on the inner dimensions of faith, from 'Mysteries of Prayer'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "ولذلك جمع الله تعالى بينهما في وصف من أثنى عليهم فقال تعالى: ﴿تَتَجَافَىٰ جُنُوبُهُمْ عَنِ الْمَضَاجِعِ يَدْعُونَ رَبَّهُمْ خَوْفًا وَطَمَعًا وَمِمَّا رَزَقْنَاهُمْ يُنْفِقُونَ﴾ وقال عز وجل: ﴿وَيَدْعُونَنَا رَغَبًا وَرَهَبًا﴾ وأين مثل عمر رضي الله عنه فالخلق الموجودة في هذا الزمان كلهم الأصلح لهم غلبة الخوف بشرط أن لا يخرجهم إلى اليأس وترك العمل وقطع الطمع من المغفرة", "english": "And therefore Allah the Exalted combined them in describing those He praised, so He said: 'Their sides forsake their beds, calling upon their Lord in fear (Khauf) and hope (Tama'a), and from what We have provided them they spend.' And He the Mighty and Majestic said: 'And they call upon Us in desire (Raghban) and fear (Rahban).' And where is the like of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him? The created beings existing in this time, all of them, the most appropriate for them is the predominance of fear (Khauf) on condition that it does not lead them to despair (Yas) and abandoning work (Amal) and cutting off hope (Tama'a) from forgiveness, for that is despair (Qunut) and is not fear (Khauf).", "book_file": "vol4_Vol4-book3.doc.txt", "book_title": "Fear and Hope", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's insights on the inner dimensions of faith, from 'Fear and Hope'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "ثم انظر كيف وصف الفائزين من عباده وبماذا وصفهم فقال تعالى: ﴿ أمن هو قانت آناء الليل ساجدا وقائما يحذر الآخرة ويرجو رحمة ربه قل هل يستوي الذين يعلمون والذين لا يعلمون ﴾ {} وقال تعالى: ﴿ تتجافى جنوبهم عن المضاجع يدعون ربهم خوفا وطمعا ﴾ {} وقال عز وجل: ﴿ والذين يبيتون لربهم سجدا وقياما ﴾ {} وقال عز وجل: ﴿ كانوا قليلا من الليل ما يهجعون وبالأسحار هم يستغفرون ﴾ {} وقال عز وجل: ﴿ فسبحان الله حين تمسون وحين تصبحون ﴾ {} وقال تعالى: ﴿ ولا تطرد الذين يدعون ربهم بالغداة والعشي يريدون وجهه ﴾ {}. فهذا كله يب", "english": "Then look at how He described the successful ones among His servants and with what He described them, so He the Exalted said: 'Is one who is devoutly obedient (Qānit) during periods of the night, prostrating and standing [in prayer], fearing the Hereafter and hoping for the mercy of his Lord [like one who does not]? Say, \"Are those who know equal to those who do not know?\"' And He the Exalted said: 'Their sides (Junūb) forsake (Tatajāfā) [their] beds as they call upon their Lord in fear (Khawf) and hope (Ṭamaʿ), and from what We have provided them, they spend.' And He the Mighty and Majestic said: 'And those who spend [part of] the night to their Lord prostrating and standing [in prayer].' And He the Mighty and Majestic said: 'They used to sleep but little of the night, and in the hours before dawn they would ask forgiveness.' And He the Mighty and Majestic said: 'So exalted is Allah when you reach the evening and when you reach the morning.' And He the Exalted said: 'And do not drive away those who call upon their Lord morning and evening, seeking His countenance (Wajh).' All of this clarifies for you that the path to the Exalted is monitoring the times and populating them with regular devotional acts (Awrād) on the basis of continuity, and for that reason he (the Prophet) said: 'The most beloved of Allah's servants to Allah are those who observe the sun, the moon, and the shadows for the Remembrance (Dhikr) of Allah the Exalted.'", "book_file": "vol1_Vol1-book-10.doc.txt", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the virtue and practice of remembering God, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Dhikr"}, {"arabic": "[42] حديث سئل عن قوله تعالى تتجافى جنوبهم عن المضاجع فقال الصلاة بين العشاءين ثم قال عليكم بالصلاة بين العشاءين فإنها تذهب بملاغات النهار وتهذب آخره قال المصنف أسنده ابن أبي الزناد [قول العراقى ابن أبى الزناد هى نسخة وقعت له وإلا ففى النسخ الصحيحة ابن أبى الزياد فليتأمل] إلى رسول الله ﭬ قلت إنما هو إسماعيل بن أبي زياد بالياء المثناة من تحت رواه أبو منصور الديلمي في مسند الفردوس من رواية إسماعيل بن أبي زياد الشامي عن الأعمش حدثنا أبو العلاء العنبري عن سلمان قال قال رسول الله ﭬ عليكم بالالصلاة بين", "english": "[42] A hadith: He was asked about His saying, the Most High: 'Their sides (جنوبهم) withdraw from the beds (المضاجع), they call upon their Lord in fear (خوفًا) and hope (طمعًا), and from what We have provided them they spend.' He said: The prayer between the two evening prayers (العشاءين). Then he said: Upon you is the prayer between the two evening prayers, for indeed it removes the frivolities of the day and refines its end. The author said: Ibn Abi Zinad attributed it [the commentator says: Ibn Abi Zinad is a copy that came to him, otherwise in the correct copies it is Ibn Abi Ziyad, so let it be considered] to the Messenger of God, peace be upon him. I say: Rather it is Isma'il ibn Abi Ziyad with the dotted Ya. Abu Mansur al-Daylami narrated it in Musnad al-Firdaws from the narration of Isma'il ibn Abi Ziyad al-Shami from al-A'mash: Abu al-'Ala al-'Anbari narrated to us from Salman, he said: The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, said: 'Upon you is the prayer between the two evening prayers, for indeed it removes the frivolities of the beginning of the day and refines its end.' And this Isma'il is abandoned, he fabricates hadith, al-Daraqutni said it. And the name of Abi Ziyad is Muslim. And there is disagreement about it from al-A'mash. And for Ibn Mardawayh from the hadith of Anas that it was revealed concerning the prayer between Maghrib and 'Isha'. And the hadith is with al-Tirmidhi and he graded it as good with the wording: 'It was revealed concerning waiting for the prayer which is called al-'Atmah (the late evening prayer).'", "book_file": "vol1_Vol1-book-10.doc.txt", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions cited by Al-Ghazali with scholarly authentication, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "ولذلك قال تعالى: ﴿ ويدعوننا رغبا ورهبا ﴾ {} وقال عز وجلّ: ﴿ يدعون ربهم خوفا وطمعا ﴾ {} ولذلك عبر العرب عن الخوف بالرجاء فقال تعالى: ﴿ ما لكم لا ترجون لله وقارا ﴾ {} أي لا تخافون وكثيرا ما ورد في القرآن الرجاء بمعنى الخوف وذلك لتلازمهما إذ عادة العرب التعبير عن الشيء بما يلازمه", "english": "And therefore Allah the Exalted said: 'And they call upon Us in hope and fear' (Quran 21:90) and the Almighty said: 'Their sides forsake their beds, calling upon their Lord in fear and hope (طمعا)' (Quran 32:16). And therefore the Arabs expressed fear (الخوف) through hope (الرجاء) as Allah said: 'Why do you not hope (ترجون) for Allah with reverence?' (Quran 71:13) meaning 'Why do you not fear?' And often in the Quran hope (الرجاء) comes with the meaning of fear (الخوف), and that is due to their inseparability, for it is the custom of the Arabs to express a thing through what accompanies it.", "book_file": "vol4_Vol4-book3.doc.txt", "book_title": "Fear and Hope", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's insights on the inner dimensions of faith, from 'Fear and Hope'", "badge": "Insight"}], "17": [{"arabic": "وقيل في قوله تعالى: ﴿ فلا تعلم نفس ما أخفي لهم من قرة أعين جزاء بما كانوا يعملون ﴾ قيل كان عملهم الصيام لأنه قال: ﴿ إنما يوفى الصابرون أجرهم بغير حساب ﴾ فيفرغ للصائم جزاؤه إفراغا ويجازف جزافا فلا يدخل تحت وهم وتقدير وجدير بأن يكون كذلك لأن الصوم إنما كان له ومشرفا بالنسبة إليه وإن كانت العبادات كلها له كما شرف البيت بالنسبة إلى نفسه والأرض كلها له لمعنيين: أحدهما أن الصوم كف وترك وهو في نفسه سر ليس فيه عمل يشاهد وجميع أعمال الطاعات بمشهد من الخلق ومرأى والصوم لا يراه إلا الله عز وجل فإنه عمل في ", "english": "And it is said regarding His statement, the Exalted: 'So no soul (Nafs) knows what has been hidden for them of delight of the eyes as reward for what they used to do' (Quran 32:17), it is said their action was fasting because He said: 'Indeed, the patient ones will be given their reward without account' (Quran 39:10). So the reward for the fasting person is poured out completely and given generously, not entering under imagination and estimation, and it is worthy to be so because fasting is specifically for Him and honored in relation to Him, even though all acts of worship are for Him, just as the House is honored in relation to Himself and all the earth is His, for two meanings: The first is that fasting is restraint (Kaf) and abandonment (Tark), and it is in itself a secret (Sirr) with no visible action, and all acts of obedience are witnessed and seen by creation, but fasting is not seen except by Allah, the Exalted, for it is an action in the inner (Batin) with pure patience (Sabr). The second is that it subdues the enemy of Allah, the Exalted, for the means of Satan, may Allah curse him, are the desires (Shahawat), and desires are strengthened by eating and drinking, and therefore the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: 'Indeed, Satan flows in the son of Adam like blood, so constrict his pathways with hunger.' And therefore the Prophet, peace be upon him, said to Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her: 'Persist in knocking on the door of Paradise.' She said: 'With what?' He said, peace be upon him: 'With hunger.' And the virtue of hunger will come in the book on the evil of food and its treatment from the quarter of the destroyers. Since fasting in particular is suppression of Satan, blocking of his paths, and constriction of his pathways, it deserves specification in relation to Allah, the Exalted, for in suppressing the enemy of Allah is victory for Allah, glory be to Him, and the supporter of Allah, the Exalted, is dependent on victory for Him, said Allah, the Exalted.", "book_file": "vol1_Vol1-book-6.doc.txt", "book_title": "Mysteries of Fasting", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's insights on the inner dimensions of faith, from 'Mysteries of Fasting'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "ولعلها أرادت بحب الهوى: حب الله لإحسانه إليها وإنعامه عليها بحظوظ العاجلة، وبحبه لما هو أهل له: الحب لجماله وجلاله الذي انكشف لها؛ وهو أعلى الجبين وأقواهما، ولذة مطالعة جمال الربوبية هي التي عبر عنها رسول الله ﭬ حيث قال حاكياً عن ربه تعالى: \"أعددت لعبادي الصالحين ما لا عين رأت ولا أذن سمعت ولا خطر على قلب بشر\" وقد تعجل بعض هذه اللذات في الدنيا لمن انتهى صفاء قلبه إلى الغاية، ولذلك قال بعضهم: إني أقول يا رب يا الله فأجد ذلك على قلبي أثقل من الجبال لأن النداء يكون من وراء حجاب؛ وهل رأيت جليساً ينا", "english": "And perhaps she intended by love of desire (Hawa): the love of Allah for His benevolence to her and His bestowal upon her with the shares of the immediate, and by His love for that which He is worthy of: the love for His beauty and majesty which was unveiled to her; and it is the highest and strongest of the two, and the delight of contemplating the beauty of Lordship (Rububiyyah) is that which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ expressed when he said, narrating from his Lord, the Exalted: \"I have prepared for My righteous servants what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and has not occurred to the heart of a human.\" And some of these delights are hastened in this world for one whose purity of heart (Qalb) has reached the ultimate, and for that reason some of them said: I say, O Lord, O Allah, and I find that upon my heart heavier than the mountains because the call is from behind a veil; and have you seen a companion calling his companion? And he said: When a man reaches the ultimate in this knowledge, the creation pelts him with stones; meaning his speech goes beyond the limit of their intellects so they see what he says as madness or disbelief, so the aim of all the knowers (Arifin) is only His connection and meeting, so it is the coolness of the eye (Qurrat al-Ayn) which no soul (Nafs) knows what has been hidden for them of it, and when it is attained, all worries and desires are obliterated and the heart becomes immersed in its bliss, so if he were thrown into the Fire, he would not feel it due to his immersion, and if the bliss of Paradise were presented to him, he would not turn to it due to the perfection of his bliss and his reaching the ultimate which has nothing above it, and I wish I knew how one who only understands the love of tangible things believes in the delight of looking at the Face of Allah, the Exalted, when He has no image nor form? And what meaning is there to Allah's promise of it to His servants and His mention that it is the greatest of blessings? Rather, whoever knows Allah knows that the delights scattered by different desires all fold under this delight as some of them said:", "book_file": "vol4_Vol4-book6.doc.txt", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's insights on the inner dimensions of faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وقد شرح الله الرجاء فقال \"إن الذين آمنوا والذين هاجروا وجاهدوا في سبيل الله أولئك يرجون رحمة الله\" يعني أن الرجاء بهم أليق وهذا لأنه ذكر أن ثواب الآخرة أجر وجزاء على الأعمال قال الله تعالى \"جزاء بما كانوا يعملون\" وقال تعالى \"وإنما توفون أجوركم يوم القيامة\" أفترى أن من استؤجر على إصلاح أوان وشرط له أجرة عليها وكان الشارط كريماً يفي بالوعد مهما وعد ولا يخلف بل يزيد، فجاء الأجير وكسر الأواني وأفسد جميعها ثم جلس ينتظر الأجر ويزعم أن المستأجر كريم، أفيراه العقلاء في انتظاره متمنياً مغروراً أو راجياً؟", "english": "And Allah has explained hope (al-raja') by saying: 'Indeed, those who have believed and those who have emigrated and striven in the path of Allah - those expect the mercy of Allah.' This means that hope (al-raja') is more appropriate for them, and this is because He mentioned that the reward of the Hereafter is a wage and recompense for deeds. Allah the Exalted said: 'As recompense for what they used to do.' And He the Exalted said: 'And you will only be given your rewards on the Day of Resurrection.' Do you think that if someone is hired to repair vessels and is promised a wage for it, and the promiser is generous, fulfilling whatever he promises without failing but rather increasing, then the hired worker comes and breaks the vessels and ruins all of them, then sits waiting for the wage and claims that the hirer is generous - would the people of intellect see him in his waiting as a wishful person (mutamannin) deluded (maghrur) or as a hopeful person (raji)?", "book_file": "vol3_Vol3-book-10a.doc.txt", "book_title": "Condemnation of Delusion", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on death, the grave, and preparation for the Hereafter, from 'Condemnation of Delusion'", "badge": "Afterlife"}, {"arabic": "وَكَمَا أَنَّ لَذَّةَ الرِّيَاسَةِ وَالْكَرَامَةِ أَغْلَبُ اللَّذَّاتِ عَلَى مَنْ جَاوَزَ نَقْصَانَ الصِّبَا وَالْعَتْهِ فَلَذَّةُ مَعْرِفَةِ اللَّهِ تَعَالَى وَمُطَالَعَةِ جَمَالِ حَضْرَةِ الرُّبُوبِيَّةِ وَالنَّظَرِ إِلَى أَسْرَارِ الْأُمُورِ الْإِلَهِيَّةِ أَلَذُّ مِنَ الرِّيَاسَةِ الَّتِي هِيَ أَعْلَى اللَّذَّاتِ الْغَالِبَةِ عَلَى الْخَلْقِ، وَغَايَةُ الْعِبَارَةِ عَنْهُ أَنْ يُقَالَ \"فَلَا تَعْلَمُ نَفْسٌ مَا أُخْفِيَ لَهُمْ مِنْ قُرَّةِ أَعْيُنٍ\" وَأَنَّهُ أَعَدَّ لَهُمْ مَا لَا عَيْنٌ رَ", "english": "And just as the pleasure (Ladhdha) of leadership (Riyasah) and honor (Karāmah) is the most dominant of pleasures for one who has surpassed the deficiency of childhood (Sibā) and foolishness (Atah), so the pleasure (Ladhdha) of the knowledge (Ma'rifah) of Allah, the Exalted, and the contemplation (Mutāla'ah) of the beauty (Jamāl) of the presence (Hadhrah) of Lordship (Rubūbiyyah) and the observation (Nadhar) of the secrets (Asrār) of divine (Ilāhiyyah) matters is more pleasurable than leadership (Riyasah), which is the highest of the pleasures that dominate over creation (Khalq). The ultimate expression about it is to say: 'So no soul (Nafs) knows what has been hidden for them of the coolness (Qurrah) of eyes (A'yun) as a reward (Jazā') for what they used to do.' And that He has prepared for them what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and has not occurred to the heart (Qalb) of any human (Bashar). This now is not known except by one who has tasted both pleasures, for he inevitably prefers seclusion (Tabattul) and solitude (Tafarruḍ) and reflection (Fikr) and remembrance (Dhikr) and immerses himself in the seas of knowledge (Ma'rifah) and abandons leadership (Riyasah) and considers contemptible the creation (Khalq) whom he leads, due to his knowledge ('Ilm) of the annihilation (Fanā') of his leadership and the annihilation of those over whom his leadership is, and its being mixed with the turbidities (Kadūrāt) from which freedom (Khulū) is inconceivable, and its being cut off by death (Mawt) which must inevitably come, no matter how the earth takes its adornment and becomes beautified and its people think they are capable of it. So he considers great, in comparison to it, the pleasure (Ladhdha) of the knowledge (Ma'rifah) of Allah and the contemplation (Mutāla'ah) of His attributes (Sifāt) and actions (Af'āl) and the order (Nidhām) of His kingdom (Mamlakah) from the highest 'Illiyyīn to the lowest of the low (Asfal al-Sāfilīn), for it is free from competitors (Mazāhimāt) and disturbances (Makdirāt), spacious for those who come to it, not constricted for them by its greatness. Its width ('Arḍ) in terms of estimation (Taqdīr) is the heavens and the earth, and when observation (Nadhar) goes beyond the estimated things (Muqaddarāt), there is no end to its width. So the knower ('Ārif) by contemplating it is in a garden (Jannah) whose width is the heavens and the earth, grazing in its meadows (Riyāḍ), plucking from its fruits (Thimār), and drinking from its pools (Hiyāḍ), secure from its cessation, for the fruits of this garden are not cut off nor forbidden. Then it is eternal (Abadiyyah) and everlasting (Sarmadiyyah), not cut off by death (Mawt), for death does not destroy the place (Mahall) of the knowledge (Ma'rifah) of Allah, the Exalted, and its place is the spirit (Rūḥ) which is a divine (Rabbānī) celestial (Samāwī) command (Amr). Then", "book_file": "vol4_Vol4-book6.doc.txt", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's insights on the inner dimensions of faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وقال الله تعالى: \" ولدينا مزيد \". قال بعض المفسرين: يأتي أهل الجنة في وقت المزيد ثلاث تحف من عند رب العالمين؛ أحداها: هدية من عند الله تعالى ليس عندهم في الجنان مثلها فذلك قوله تعالى: \" فلا تعلم نفس ما أخفي لهم من قرة أعين \" والثانية: السلام عليهم من ربهم، فيزيد ذلك على الهدية فضلاً وهو قوله تعالى: \" سلام قولاً من رب رحيم \" والثالثة: يقول الله تعالى: إني عنكم راض فيكون ذلك أفضل من الهدية والتسليم فذلك قوله تعالى: \" ورضوان من الله أكبر \" أي من النعيم الذي هم فيه فهذا فضل رضا الله تعالى وهو ثمرة ر", "english": "And Allah the Exalted said: \"And with Us is more.\" Some interpreters said: The people of Paradise will come at the time of increase with three gifts from the Lord of the worlds; the first: a gift from Allah the Exalted that they do not have in the Gardens like it, and that is His saying the Exalted: \"So no Soul (Nafs) knows what has been hidden for them of delight of the eyes\" and the second: Peace upon them from their Lord, and that increases over the gift in favor, and it is His saying the Exalted: \"Peace, a word from a Lord Most Merciful\" and the third: Allah the Exalted says: I am pleased with you, so that becomes better than the gift and the greeting, and that is His saying the Exalted: \"And Pleasure (Ridwan) from Allah is greater\" meaning than the bliss they are in, so this is the favor of the Pleasure (Ridwan) of Allah the Exalted, and it is the fruit of the pleasure (Rida) of the servant.", "book_file": "vol4_Vol4-book6.doc.txt", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's insights on the inner dimensions of faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Insight"}], "20": [{"arabic": "ثم ليحضر بعد أهوال القيامة في قلبه صورة جهنم ودركاتها ومقامعها وأهوالها وسلاسلها وأغلالها وزقومها وصديدها وأنواع العذاب فيها وقبح صور الزبانية الموكلين بها وأنهم كلما نضجت جلودهم بدلوا جلودا غيرها وأنهم كلما أرادوا أن يخرجوا منها أعيدوا فيها وأنهم إذا رأوها من مكان بعيد سمعوا لها تغيظا وزفيرا وهلم جرا إلى جميع ما ورد في القرآن من شرحها", "english": "Then let him present in his heart (Qalb) after the terrors of the Resurrection the image of Hell (Jahannam) and its depths, its beatings, its terrors, its chains, its shackles, its Zaqqum, its pus, and the types of torment in it, and the ugliness of the forms of the guardians (Zabaniyah) appointed over it, and that whenever their skins are roasted, they are replaced with other skins, and that whenever they desire to exit from it, they are returned into it, and that when they see it from a distant place, they hear for it raging and roaring, and so on to all that has come in the Quran of its explanation.", "book_file": "vol4_j4-k09.doc.txt", "book_title": "Contemplation", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's insights on the inner dimensions of faith, from 'Contemplation'", "badge": "Insight"}], "24": [{"arabic": "وقد وصف الله تعالى الصابرين بأوصاف وذكر الصبر في القرآن في نيف وسبعين موضعا وأضاف أكثر الدرجات والخيرات إلى الصبر وجعلها ثمرة له فقال عز من قائل: ﴿ وجعلنا منهم أئمة يهدون بأمرنا لما صبروا ﴾ {} وقال تعالى: ﴿ وتمت كلمة ربك الحسنى على بني إسرائيل بما صبروا ﴾ {} وقال تعالى: ﴿ ولنجزين الذين صبروا أجرهم بأحسن ما كانوا يعملون ﴾ {} وقال تعالى: ﴿ أولئك يؤتون أجرهم مرتين بما صبروا ﴾ {} وقال تعالى: ﴿ إنما يوفى الصابرون أجرهم بغير حساب ﴾ {} فما من قربة إلا وأجرها بتقدير وحساب إلا الصبر ولأجل كون الصوم من ال", "english": "And Allah the Exalted has described the patient ones with attributes and mentioned patience in the Quran in over seventy places, and He attributed most degrees and goodnesses to patience and made them its fruit, as He the Mighty and Majestic said: 'And We made from among them leaders guiding by Our command when they were patient' (Quran 32:24), and He the Exalted said: 'And the good word of your Lord was fulfilled upon the Children of Israel because they were patient' (Quran 7:137), and He the Exalted said: 'And We will surely give those who were patient their reward by the best of what they used to do' (Quran 16:96), and He the Exalted said: 'Those will be given their reward twice for what they were patient' (Quran 28:54), and He the Exalted said: 'Indeed, the patient ones will be given their reward without account' (Quran 39:10). So there is no act of worship except that its reward is by measure and account except patience, and because fasting is from patience and it is half of patience, Allah the Exalted said: 'Fasting is for Me and I will reward it' (Hadith), so He attributed it to Himself from among all other acts of worship, and He promised the patient ones that He is with them, as He the Exalted said: 'And be patient, indeed Allah is with the patient ones' (Quran 8:46), and He made victory conditional upon patience, as He the Exalted said: 'Yes, if you are patient and fear (Allah) and they come upon you in this heat, your Lord will reinforce you with five thousand angels having marks' (Quran 3:125), and He gathered for the patient ones between matters He did not gather for others, as He the Exalted said: 'Those are upon them prayers from their Lord and mercy, and those are the guided ones' (Quran 2:157). So guidance, mercy, and prayers are gathered for the patient ones, and enumerating all the verses in the station of patience would be lengthy.", "book_file": "vol4_Vol4-book2.doc.txt", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's insights on the inner dimensions of faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}]}, "wordTiming": {"abdulbasit": {"1": [[1, 350, 7342]], "2": [[1, 350, 1381], [2, 1381, 2299], [3, 2299, 2786], [4, 2786, 3442], [5, 3442, 4079], [6, 4079, 4454], [7, 4454, 5110], [8, 5110, 6558]], "3": [[1, 400, 883], [2, 883, 2573], [3, 2573, 4022], [4, 4022, 4461], [5, 4461, 4988], [6, 4988, 5976], [7, 5976, 6415], [8, 6415, 7403], [9, 7403, 8523], [10, 8523, 9687], [11, 9687, 11138], [12, 11138, 12565], [1